{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793572835,"nonce":"xwzceZokGhYNYTToXcpa2JMY7Ow=","title":"scimark sparsematmult runs 6% slower in tiered mode with the jump_thread pass present","message":"NUC, 4 pinned cores, ZYPY_LLVM=1, `scimark.py -n 1 --benchmark=SparseMatMult 1000 50000 2000`, interleaved, 6 to 8 reps: a build with the pass called but returning at once, or never called, 112 to 114 ms median; a build with the pass active 120 to 122 ms. The Cranelift-only run is the same for both (38.6 ms first iteration), and the tiered steady-state iteration is the same (16 ms per 600 cycles); the difference is in the first 100 ms, before the optimising tier takes over. Over the first three iterations of an 8-iteration run the totals are equal, so work moves between iterations.\n\nWhat was ruled out: the pass changes no function in the run (ZYNTAX_TRACE_JUMP_THREAD names only zb_hook_shaped_items, and skipping that function leaves the slowdown), its time is 0.4 ms, portable placement is the same (155 functions), the OSR probe and publish sequence is the same, the LLVM IR of the promoted functions is the same modulo instruction order and runtime addresses, and the pass's own result with it disabled by environment variable cannot be compared (any ZYNTAX_* variable turns portable code off, see 3bdfe63's notes). Suspects: code layout of the compiler or library changing the timing of the tier-up race, or instruction order in the snapshot's library bodies.\n\nSuite A/B of the pass (5 reps, 22 kernels, geomean wall 0.978) is the right measure; this row is an outlier in it.","files":null}]}